<p>## 6. The <code>MERGE</code> Procedure</p>
<p>The crucial operation in Merge Sort is the “combine” step, handled by an auxiliary procedure called <code>MERGE(A, p, q, r)</code>.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Function:</strong> It assumes that the two subarrays <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A%5Bp%20%5Cdots%20q%5D"> and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A%5Bq+1%20%5Cdots%20r%5D"> are already sorted. It merges them into a single sorted subarray that replaces <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A%5Bp%20%5Cdots%20r%5D">.</li>
<li><strong>Card Analogy:</strong> Imagine two sorted piles of cards face up on a table. You compare the top two cards, remove the smaller one, and place it face down in a new output pile. You repeat this constant-time comparison step until all cards are merged.</li>
<li><strong>Time Complexity:</strong> Because each basic step compares two cards and takes constant time, merging <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?n"> total elements takes <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5CTheta(n)"> time.</li>
<li><strong>Sentinels:</strong> To prevent the algorithm from constantly needing to check if either pile is completely empty, a special “sentinel” card containing the value infinity (<img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cinfty">) is placed at the bottom of each pile. This guarantees that the core sorting logic stops exactly when all real data has been merged.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
</section>
