<section id="understanding-the-mergea-p-q-r-procedure" class="level3">
<h3 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="understanding-the-mergea-p-q-r-procedure">Understanding the <code>MERGE(A, p, q, r)</code> Procedure</h3>
<p>To understand exactly how this pseudocode works, let’s trace it using a concrete example.</p>
<section id="our-setup" class="level4">
<h4 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="our-setup">Our Setup</h4>
<p>Imagine we have an array <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A"> (using 1-based indexing, as the textbook does):</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AA%20=%20%5B8,%204,%207,%202,%205,%209%5D%0A"></p>
<p>We want to merge two sorted sections in the middle of this array:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The left sorted pile:</strong> <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5B4,%207%5D"> (located at indices 2 and 3)</li>
<li><strong>The right sorted pile:</strong> <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5B2,%205%5D"> (located at indices 4 and 5)</li>
</ul>
<p>We call <code>MERGE(A, 2, 3, 5)</code>. Here, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?p%20=%202"> (start), <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?q%20=%203"> (middle), and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?r%20=%205"> (end).</p>
<hr>
</section>
